# `CF412_TRIAL=<steps>` runs the whole pipeline at a few tens of backbone
# steps. Its score is a wiring check, not a measurement, and a trial file
# beside the study's own results is one glob away from being read as a study
# number.
#
# Three files are the exception, and they are committed. `smoke.csv` and
# `smoke.log` carry the shape each arm's trainer actually ran, the
# depth-column count, the memory and the step time the run plan is sized from.
# `trial.log` carries the end-to-end pass through the head and the
# evaluation.
results/trial/*
!results/trial/smoke.csv
!results/trial/smoke.log
!results/trial/trial.log

# `run_snapshot/` is a frozen copy of `scripts/`, and a lane runs from it.
#
# Two agent sessions share this checkout. On 2026-09-06 one edited
# `run_arm.sh` and `phase1.sh` in place while the other's lanes were running
# them. Bash reads a script by byte offset and re-reads the file after each
# command, so a running shell resumed inside the new text and died on a syntax
# error. That cost `k3_r100_09` its 100,000-step head.
#
# A lane launched from a frozen copy cannot be edited under itself.
# `study.sh` takes the study directory from its own parent, so a copy that
# sits beside `scripts/` writes to the same `results/` and `plots/`.
#
# It is a copy of committed files, so it is not committed again.
run_snapshot/

# `results/lane_d.log` is not committed. Two sessions opened it at once on
# 2026-09-07, one with `>`, so its text interleaves at two offsets. Every line
# it held is also in `results/arms.log` and `results/phase1.log`, which append.
results/lane_d.log
